{%- comment -%}
Index builder for a Jekyll site.

Jekyll builds it as it builds the site, so the index cannot drift from the text
and needs no separate step: no npm, no action, no remembering to rebuild. The
format is the shared one (see FORMAT.md); the engine knows nothing of Jekyll.

The unit is a paragraph of the source, that is, a piece between blank lines.
Paragraphs are grouped by page so as not to repeat its address and title for
each one.

There is nothing in Liquid to cut on a blank line except to mark the line ends
with newline_to_br and split on a pair of "<br />". The marks are taken off
afterwards: wherever two blank lines met, a spare "<br />" is left at the head of
a chunk, and markdown then takes it for the start of a paragraph — the heading
below it stops being a heading and travels into the results hashes and all.

Markup is stripped by markdownify, which turns a paragraph into HTML, and by
strip_html, which turns that into bare text. Doing it here rather than in the
browser is the whole point of a shared format: the engine receives text, not
somebody's source. The price is that kramdown escapes "&" and the quotes, so
five entities have to be put back by hand — Liquid has no regular expressions.
Surviving "**" too: the source holds the occasional "**word **", which is not
emphasis to markdown, and it leaves the asterisks as they stand.

strip_html runs before markdownify as well as after, and that is not belt and
braces. Jekyll hands over page.content already rendered for some pages —
which ones depends on the order it happens to build them in — and a chunk cut
out of finished HTML is a fragment: an open tag whose close went to the next
chunk, the tail of a code block. Kramdown cannot parse a fragment, so it
escapes it instead, strip_html then sees no tags at all, and putting the five
entities back brings the markup out into the reader's results. Six per cent of
the paragraphs of one index arrived that way. Stripping first leaves kramdown
nothing to give up on. The cell separators are inserted twice for the same
reason: once for a table that arrives as HTML, once for one that arrives as
markdown.

Table cells would be glued into one word by strip_html —
"TermSanskritMeaning" — so each is given a separator beforehand (the closing
bracket in the pattern is there so that "<th" does not catch "<thead" as well),
and the spare one that ends up before the first is taken off at the end — but
only if it really does stand first: remove_first cuts the first match wherever
it is, and a separator dot lives in the text on its own account
("Search the site · First principles"), which must not be taken away. An empty
cell leaves two separators in a row; a pair of passes reduces them to one, and
more than two empty cells running do not occur in these tables.

Whitespace is collapsed by "split: ' ' | join: ' '": in Ruby, splitting on a
single space is the special case that cuts on any run of whitespace, newlines
included. A non-breaking space does not fall under that — to Ruby it is not
whitespace — and has to be named on its own; in these sources a whole indent is
sometimes made of them.

What is configurable lives in _data/sitesearch.yml: the names of the sections by
the first segment of the address, and their order in the results. That is about
the content of a site rather than the mechanics, and so stays with the site.

A page can be left out with "search: false" in its front matter — that is how
the search page itself is left out. A single piece of furniture is left out with
the class nosearch. Breadcrumbs, the pager and a page's own table of contents
are made of links and give nothing but noise in the results; they are never
identical, though (each carries the names of its own neighbours), so the
repeat-dropping the engine can do will not remove them.

Only .md pages go into the index. Viewers and other .html carry their own styles
and scripts, nine tenths of their text is CSS and JS, and in the results there
is nothing but noise to be had from it.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- assign conf = site.data.sitesearch -%}
{%- capture sep %}<br />
<br />{% endcapture -%}
{"pages":[
{%- assign firstpage = true -%}
{%- for p in site.pages -%}
{%- unless p.path contains '.md' -%}{% continue %}{% endunless -%}
{%- if p.search == false -%}{% continue %}{% endif -%}
{%- if p.path contains 'sitesearch/' -%}{% continue %}{% endif -%}
{%- assign parts = p.url | split: '/' -%}
{%- assign seg = parts[1] -%}
{%- assign sec = conf.sections[seg] -%}
{%- assign sectitle = sec.title | default: conf.default.title -%}
{%- assign secorder = sec.order | default: conf.default.order | default: 99 -%}
{%- unless firstpage %},{% endunless -%}
{%- assign firstpage = false -%}
{"url":{{ p.url | jsonify }},"title":{{ p.title | default: p.url | jsonify }},"section":{{ sectitle | jsonify }},"order":{{ secorder }},"blocks":[
{%- assign firstpara = true -%}
{%- assign chunks = p.content | newline_to_br | split: sep -%}
{%- for chunk in chunks -%}
{%- if chunk contains 'nosearch' -%}{% continue %}{% endif -%}
{%- assign text = chunk | replace: '<br />', '' | replace: '<td>', ' · <td>' | replace: '<td ', ' · <td ' | replace: '<th>', ' · <th>' | replace: '<th ', ' · <th ' | strip_html | markdownify | replace: '<td>', ' · <td>' | replace: '<td ', ' · <td ' | replace: '<th>', ' · <th>' | replace: '<th ', ' · <th ' | strip_html | replace: '&amp;', '&' | replace: '&lt;', '<' | replace: '&gt;', '>' | replace: '&quot;', '"' | replace: '&#39;', "'" | replace: '**', '' | replace: '&nbsp;', ' ' | replace: ' ', ' ' | split: ' ' | join: ' ' | replace: '· · ', '· ' | replace: '· · ', '· ' -%}
{%- assign lead = text | slice: 0, 2 -%}
{%- if lead == '· ' %}{% assign text = text | remove_first: '· ' %}{% endif -%}
{%- if text.size > 16 -%}
{%- unless firstpara %},{% endunless -%}
{%- assign firstpara = false -%}
{{ text | jsonify }}
{%- endif -%}
{%- endfor -%}
]}
{%- endfor -%}
]}
